1
00:00:04,519 --> 00:00:08,642
Engineering asks a coding agent to build a headcount
planning tool.

2
00:00:08,642 --> 00:00:15,014
TrustGraph checks two permissions: what the agent can
read, and who can access the code it creates.

3
00:00:16,441 --> 00:00:21,126
First, Rust checks the agent’s identity against each
source’s access list.

4
00:00:21,126 --> 00:00:24,108
No permission means no access before generation.

5
00:00:24,108 --> 00:00:28,794
In this task, the agent is allowed to read HR salaries.

6
00:00:30,328 --> 00:00:34,392
The task arrives through MCP or the local API.

7
00:00:34,392 --> 00:00:39,360
TrustGraph starts a fresh Codex run with permitted data
and instructions.

8
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:43,424
Other tools are disabled, keeping that run’s inputs
controlled.

9
00:00:45,067 --> 00:00:52,554
TrustGraph writes evidence into HydraDB: source versions,
access lists, the task, recorded inputs, and

10
00:00:52,554 --> 00:00:53,624
generated code.

11
00:00:53,624 --> 00:00:56,298
Content fingerprints identify exact records.

12
00:00:56,298 --> 00:01:01,646
Links connect the sources to the run and its output.

13
00:01:03,634 --> 00:01:09,335
To check a release, TrustGraph reads records and links
back from HydraDB.

14
00:01:09,335 --> 00:01:13,136
It verifies fingerprints and rebuilds the source history.

15
00:01:13,136 --> 00:01:15,987
Missing or changed evidence blocks release.

16
00:01:17,738 --> 00:01:23,020
Rust follows every input and keeps the people allowed by
all of them.

17
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:25,458
Salaries allow HR, but not Engineering.

18
00:01:25,458 --> 00:01:30,741
Sharing this code with Engineering is blocked, with the
exact source-to-output path shown.

19
00:01:32,362 --> 00:01:36,258
HR has shared headcounts and budget bands with
Engineering.

20
00:01:36,258 --> 00:01:40,155
A fresh run uses that data and the directory.

21
00:01:40,155 --> 00:01:43,185
Salaries and the old draft stay out.

22
00:01:43,185 --> 00:01:45,783
HydraDB records a new source history.

23
00:01:47,447 --> 00:01:49,736
Engineering can access this version.

24
00:01:49,736 --> 00:01:52,482
A human reviews the exact code.

25
00:01:52,482 --> 00:01:56,145
Approval covers its fingerprint, destination, and policy
context.

26
00:01:56,145 --> 00:02:00,264
The approval and its links are saved in HydraDB.

27
00:02:01,993 --> 00:02:07,303
Before publishing, TrustGraph reads back the evidence and
checks access and approval again.

28
00:02:07,303 --> 00:02:14,654
It records the attempt in a durable local log, creates a
GitHub draft pull request, and stores the

29
00:02:14,654 --> 00:02:15,879
receipt in HydraDB.

30
00:02:17,608 --> 00:02:20,462
Edit the code and its fingerprint changes.

31
00:02:20,462 --> 00:02:22,909
The old approval no longer matches.

32
00:02:22,909 --> 00:02:28,618
Publishing the new version is blocked until a human
reviews and approves it again.

33
00:02:28,618 --> 00:02:30,658
The earlier release stays recorded.

34
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:37,354
The simulator tests proposed policies on an isolated copy
of the evidence.

35
00:02:37,354 --> 00:02:42,408
Repeat with the same data, rules and evaluation time: the
same decision.

36
00:02:42,408 --> 00:02:44,514
Test changes without publishing them.

37
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:48,312
HydraDB preserves the connected evidence.

38
00:02:48,312 --> 00:02:50,209
Rust enforces the rules.

39
00:02:50,209 --> 00:02:57,325
TrustGraph controls generation and publishing, applying
permissions both to what the agent reads and to

40
00:02:57,325 --> 00:02:59,222
the work it creates.
